1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,745
Ask an A.I. one simple question.

2
00:00:02,745 --> 00:00:04,863
Will it rain in my village tomorrow?

3
00:00:04,863 --> 00:00:08,753
Before it reads your question, the machine chops it into pieces.

4
00:00:08,753 --> 00:00:10,925
In English... just eight.

5
00:00:10,925 --> 00:00:12,670
Now ask it in Hindi.

6
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:16,111
On DeepSeek, the famous Chinese A.I.... eighteen.

7
00:00:16,111 --> 00:00:17,413
More than double.

8
00:00:17,413 --> 00:00:19,060
Marathi, twenty.

9
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:21,061
Telugu, twenty-five.

10
00:00:21,061 --> 00:00:24,601
And in those two, it even cuts some of your letters in half.

11
00:00:24,601 --> 00:00:27,593
So can India build an A.I. of its own?

12
00:00:27,593 --> 00:00:30,065
One that speaks your language best?

13
00:00:30,065 --> 00:00:31,310
Here's a clue for you.

14
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:32,713
Ask it in Chinese.

15
00:00:32,713 --> 00:00:33,951
Seven pieces.

16
00:00:33,951 --> 00:00:35,367
Fewer than English.

17
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:36,693
You see what's going on?

18
00:00:36,693 --> 00:00:40,751
A machine speaks best in the language of the people who built it.

19
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:42,222
And it's not just pride.

20
00:00:42,222 --> 00:00:45,181
Your apps pay these machines by the piece.

21
00:00:45,181 --> 00:00:49,303
More pieces means a bigger bill, and a slower answer for you.

22
00:00:49,303 --> 00:00:51,240
Now think back to last November.

23
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:56,408
Chat G.P.T.'s paid plan, called Go, became free in India for a whole year.

24
00:00:56,408 --> 00:00:57,935
Maybe you signed up too.

25
00:00:57,935 --> 00:01:01,655
OpenAI says India is now one of its biggest markets.

26
00:01:01,655 --> 00:01:06,404
For the first people who signed up, that free year runs out... about now.

27
00:01:06,404 --> 00:01:09,847
So when yours ends, whose machine will you be talking to?

28
00:01:09,847 --> 00:01:11,324
One built in America?

29
00:01:11,324 --> 00:01:12,718
One built in China?

30
00:01:12,718 --> 00:01:14,092
Or one built here?

31
00:01:14,092 --> 00:01:18,756
To see why India doesn't have its own yet, you have to go back to one Monday.

32
00:01:18,756 --> 00:01:21,765
The twenty-seventh of January, twenty twenty-five.

33
00:01:21,765 --> 00:01:26,428
A small Chinese lab called DeepSeek has just put out a free A.I.

34
00:01:26,428 --> 00:01:31,741
By that Monday, it's beaten Chat G.P.T. in downloads on Apple's App Store in America.

35
00:01:32,573 --> 00:01:39,080
DeepSeek says the final training run of its base model cost about five and a half million dollars.

36
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:43,670
The American giants, you may remember, were spending billions.

37
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:49,725
That day, the American chip company Nvidia loses around five hundred and ninety billion dollars in value.

38
00:01:49,725 --> 00:01:52,676
That's about fifty lakh crore rupees.

39
00:01:52,676 --> 00:01:54,689
Think about that number for a second.

40
00:01:54,689 --> 00:02:02,119
It's roughly the size of India's whole Union Budget for that year... gone from one company in one day.

41
00:02:02,119 --> 00:02:07,707
Three days later, in Delhi, India's I.T. minister, Ashwini Vaishnaw, makes a promise.

42
00:02:07,707 --> 00:02:13,507
India will build its own A.I. model... and he hopes it will be ready within six months.

43
00:02:13,507 --> 00:02:15,999
So did India keep that promise?

44
00:02:15,999 --> 00:02:16,944
You'll see.

45
00:02:16,944 --> 00:02:22,461
And around minute ten, I'll run that same chopping test for you again... on an Indian machine.

46
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:24,077
That's where it gets interesting.

47
00:02:24,077 --> 00:02:28,214
But first, you need to know what's actually inside one of these machines.

48
00:02:28,214 --> 00:02:30,054
Because it isn't magic.

49
00:02:30,054 --> 00:02:31,318
It's three things.

50
00:02:31,318 --> 00:02:35,406
Picture a child who has read every book in a giant library.

51
00:02:35,406 --> 00:02:38,942
You start a sentence... and the child guesses your next word.

52
00:02:38,942 --> 00:02:39,979
Then the next one.

53
00:02:39,979 --> 00:02:41,037
Then the next.

54
00:02:41,037 --> 00:02:43,276
That's really all a chatbot does.

55
00:02:43,276 --> 00:02:48,832
It guesses the next piece, again and again, so well that you feel it's thinking.

56
00:02:48,832 --> 00:02:51,964
So if you want to build one, you need three things.

57
00:02:51,964 --> 00:02:53,416
A library to read.

58
00:02:53,416 --> 00:02:55,358
A reading room where it can read.

59
00:02:55,358 --> 00:02:57,860
And teachers who know how to train the child.

60
00:02:58,745 --> 00:03:04,021
The library is words — trillions of pieces of text, like the ones you type.

61
00:03:04,021 --> 00:03:09,884
The reading room is chips, thousands of them, running day and night for months.

62
00:03:09,884 --> 00:03:11,100
And the teachers?

63
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:12,460
They're engineers.

64
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:14,080
India has the teachers.

65
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:15,463
You know this already.

66
00:03:15,463 --> 00:03:19,481
Indian engineers write software for companies all over the world.

67
00:03:19,481 --> 00:03:23,123
So your missing pieces are the library and the reading room.

68
00:03:23,123 --> 00:03:25,012
Let's start with the library.

69
00:03:25,012 --> 00:03:27,584
Almost nobody tells you about this part.

70
00:03:27,584 --> 00:03:30,441
A lot of A.I. learns from the internet.

71
00:03:30,441 --> 00:03:34,471
One of the biggest free copies of the web is called Common Crawl.

72
00:03:34,471 --> 00:03:41,012
Many A.I. models learned from it, including an early model from the makers of the Chat G.P.T. you use.

73
00:03:41,012 --> 00:03:49,821
So we took its latest copy, from October twenty twenty-six — more than two billion web pages — and checked its own count of languages.

74
00:03:49,821 --> 00:03:50,588
English?

75
00:03:50,588 --> 00:03:53,560
Forty-two out of every hundred pages.

76
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:55,497
Now, can you guess Hindi?

77
00:03:55,497 --> 00:04:00,345
For every one page in Hindi... there are about two hundred pages in English.

78
00:04:00,345 --> 00:04:04,145
And Hindi is the mother tongue of more than fifty crore people.

79
00:04:04,145 --> 00:04:04,985
Marathi?

80
00:04:04,985 --> 00:04:06,439
About fifteen hundred.

81
00:04:06,439 --> 00:04:07,161
Telugu?

82
00:04:07,161 --> 00:04:09,088
About two thousand three hundred.

83
00:04:09,088 --> 00:04:14,072
And each of those is the mother tongue of around eight crore people, maybe yours.

84
00:04:14,072 --> 00:04:19,564
All of India's languages together make up less than one page in every hundred.

85
00:04:19,564 --> 00:04:20,488
Think about it.

86
00:04:20,488 --> 00:04:25,153
More than one in every six people on Earth lives in your country.

87
00:04:25,153 --> 00:04:30,545
But in the library these machines read, your language is almost invisible.

88
00:04:30,545 --> 00:04:33,397
And India's job is harder than China's.

89
00:04:33,397 --> 00:04:36,641
China's A.I. mostly needs one written language.

90
00:04:36,641 --> 00:04:42,464
Your country's Constitution lists twenty-two, written in about a dozen different scripts.

91
00:04:42,464 --> 00:04:44,654
Each one needs its own shelf.

92
00:04:44,654 --> 00:04:49,971
And that's why your question cost so much more in Hindi, Marathi and Telugu.

93
00:04:49,971 --> 00:04:51,295
Remember the chopper?

94
00:04:51,295 --> 00:04:54,187
It learns its pieces from this same library.

95
00:04:54,187 --> 00:04:59,862
Words it sees all the time, like rain or village, become one neat piece each.

96
00:04:59,862 --> 00:05:03,906
Your words, which it hardly ever sees, get chopped into bits.

97
00:05:03,906 --> 00:05:06,018
Sometimes into half a letter.

98
00:05:06,018 --> 00:05:07,020
Here's the rule.

99
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:09,564
A machine learns from what it reads.

100
00:05:09,564 --> 00:05:10,814
And what goes wrong?

101
00:05:10,814 --> 00:05:17,552
If your language is only a tiny corner of the library, the machine learns it last... and charges you more.

102
00:05:17,552 --> 00:05:20,939
So someone has to build the Indian part of the library.

103
00:05:20,939 --> 00:05:23,367
Page by page, voice by voice.

104
00:05:23,367 --> 00:05:25,908
And that's slow, expensive work.

105
00:05:25,908 --> 00:05:27,647
And India has started.

106
00:05:27,647 --> 00:05:31,456
A government data shelf for Indian builders, called A.I.

107
00:05:31,456 --> 00:05:34,578
Kosh, now holds more than sixteen thousand collections.

108
00:05:34,578 --> 00:05:36,645
But words are only half of it.

109
00:05:36,645 --> 00:05:40,171
Even with a perfect library, you need a place to read it.

110
00:05:40,171 --> 00:05:42,591
And this is where India hits a wall.

111
00:05:42,591 --> 00:05:44,463
This is the reading room.

112
00:05:44,463 --> 00:05:48,405
A chip like this one, made by an American company called Nvidia.

113
00:05:48,405 --> 00:05:52,872
It's called a G.P.U., and a single one costs lakhs of rupees.

114
00:05:52,872 --> 00:05:58,580
And to train a big A.I., you need thousands of them, wired together, running for months.

115
00:05:58,580 --> 00:05:59,784
And here's the catch.

116
00:05:59,784 --> 00:06:01,850
India doesn't make these chips.

117
00:06:01,850 --> 00:06:05,174
They're designed in America and made mostly in Taiwan.

118
00:06:05,174 --> 00:06:07,516
If you watched our chip film, you know why.

119
00:06:08,237 --> 00:06:13,659
In January twenty twenty-five, India found out how fast that door can close.

120
00:06:13,659 --> 00:06:18,223
Just days before leaving office, America's government put out a new rule.

121
00:06:18,223 --> 00:06:20,668
It sorted the world into three groups.

122
00:06:20,668 --> 00:06:23,568
Its closest allies could buy these chips freely.

123
00:06:23,568 --> 00:06:25,180
China was blocked.

124
00:06:25,180 --> 00:06:26,243
And India?

125
00:06:26,243 --> 00:06:31,438
India was put in the middle group, with a limit on how many of these chips it could get.

126
00:06:31,438 --> 00:06:35,197
Four months later, the new American government scrapped that rule.

127
00:06:35,197 --> 00:06:36,914
But India had seen it.

128
00:06:36,914 --> 00:06:39,392
The tap is in someone else's hand.

129
00:06:39,392 --> 00:06:41,045
So what would you do?

130
00:06:41,045 --> 00:06:44,033
India didn't wait to own a giant reading room.

131
00:06:44,033 --> 00:06:46,349
It rented one... and shared it.

132
00:06:46,349 --> 00:06:51,444
Under the IndiaAI Mission, private companies put their chips into one common pool.

133
00:06:51,444 --> 00:06:56,477
Indian startups and researchers rent time on them, and the government pays part of the bill.

134
00:06:56,477 --> 00:07:01,822
In January twenty twenty-five, India lined up more than eighteen thousand chips for that pool.

135
00:07:01,822 --> 00:07:05,377
By June twenty twenty-six, more than forty-five thousand.

136
00:07:05,377 --> 00:07:06,971
That's a big jump, right?

137
00:07:06,971 --> 00:07:15,179
But by the end of twenty twenty-four, one American company, Meta, said it would have around three hundred and fifty thousand of these chips.

138
00:07:15,179 --> 00:07:19,346
Almost eight times your country's whole shared pool this June.

139
00:07:19,346 --> 00:07:20,235
And the money?

140
00:07:20,235 --> 00:07:25,574
India's whole A.I. mission is about ten thousand crore rupees, spread over five years.

141
00:07:25,574 --> 00:07:34,363
This year alone, Meta told its investors it would spend around thirteen lakh crore rupees on buildings and machines, much of it for A.I.

142
00:07:35,142 --> 00:07:39,472
So India's whole mission... ten thousand crore, over five years.

143
00:07:39,472 --> 00:07:43,822
Meta alone... thirteen lakh crore, in one single year.

144
00:07:43,822 --> 00:07:44,927
That's not double.

145
00:07:44,927 --> 00:07:47,374
That's more than a hundred times as much.

146
00:07:47,374 --> 00:07:49,201
So is it hopeless?

147
00:07:49,201 --> 00:07:49,871
No.

148
00:07:49,871 --> 00:07:51,920
Because remember DeepSeek.

149
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:56,061
DeepSeek says it trained its base model on about two thousand chips.

150
00:07:56,061 --> 00:07:57,776
Not hundreds of thousands.

151
00:07:57,776 --> 00:08:02,171
And they were a cut-down version, made so they could still be sold to China.

152
00:08:02,171 --> 00:08:04,439
So what's the lesson for you and me?

153
00:08:04,439 --> 00:08:06,888
You don't always need the biggest room.

154
00:08:06,888 --> 00:08:08,841
You need clever engineering.

155
00:08:08,841 --> 00:08:11,471
And that's a game Indian engineers can play.

156
00:08:11,471 --> 00:08:13,192
April twenty twenty-five.

157
00:08:13,192 --> 00:08:17,776
The government picks the first company to build India's own model from scratch.

158
00:08:17,776 --> 00:08:20,098
A Bengaluru startup called Sarvam.

159
00:08:20,098 --> 00:08:25,031
Sarvam was started by two Indians you should know, Vivek Raghavan and Pratyush Kumar.

160
00:08:25,031 --> 00:08:29,145
The mission gives them about four thousand of those Nvidia chips to work with.

161
00:08:29,145 --> 00:08:31,768
But a month later, the doubts begin.

162
00:08:31,768 --> 00:08:35,434
Sarvam's next model is built on top of a French one.

163
00:08:35,434 --> 00:08:39,729
And critics ask — if the base is foreign, how is it Indian?

164
00:08:39,729 --> 00:08:41,087
Fair question, isn't it?

165
00:08:41,087 --> 00:08:42,285
You'd ask it too.

166
00:08:42,285 --> 00:08:46,815
Can an Indian team train one from zero, start to finish, at home?

167
00:08:46,815 --> 00:08:48,622
February twenty twenty-six.

168
00:08:48,622 --> 00:08:50,182
Bharat Mandapam, Delhi.

169
00:08:50,182 --> 00:08:52,341
The India A.I.

170
00:08:52,341 --> 00:08:52,849
Impact Summit.

171
00:08:52,849 --> 00:08:55,308
There, Sarvam shows India two new models.

172
00:08:55,308 --> 00:08:58,974
The bigger one has a hundred and five billion parameters.

173
00:08:58,974 --> 00:09:02,933
Think of them as tiny dials the machine tunes while it reads.

174
00:09:03,639 --> 00:09:05,202
Trained from scratch.

175
00:09:05,202 --> 00:09:06,248
In India.

176
00:09:06,248 --> 00:09:08,639
On those shared chips you just saw.

177
00:09:08,639 --> 00:09:13,649
And before it ever talked to you, it read around twelve trillion pieces of text.

178
00:09:13,649 --> 00:09:16,186
Then Sarvam did something generous.

179
00:09:16,186 --> 00:09:18,094
It made the model open.

180
00:09:18,094 --> 00:09:22,932
Any Indian developer can download it and build their own apps on it, for free.

181
00:09:22,932 --> 00:09:27,460
The minister had hoped for six months, and later said ten at the most.

182
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:28,991
It took about a year.

183
00:09:28,991 --> 00:09:32,169
Late, you could say... but delivered.

184
00:09:32,169 --> 00:09:36,117
And that same week, BharatGen — a team led by I.I.T.

185
00:09:36,117 --> 00:09:42,168
Bombay — showed its own model too, called Param Two, built for all twenty-two scheduled Indian languages.

186
00:09:42,168 --> 00:09:43,901
Now remember my promise to you.

187
00:09:43,901 --> 00:09:45,200
The chopping test.

188
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:46,293
Same question.

189
00:09:46,293 --> 00:09:48,246
Will it rain in my village tomorrow?

190
00:09:48,246 --> 00:09:50,366
DeepSeek's chopper, you've seen.

191
00:09:50,366 --> 00:09:51,702
Hindi, eighteen.

192
00:09:51,702 --> 00:09:53,213
Marathi, twenty.

193
00:09:53,213 --> 00:09:54,757
Telugu, twenty-five.

194
00:09:54,757 --> 00:09:57,611
The chopper behind Chat G.P.T.'s recent models...

195
00:09:57,611 --> 00:10:01,224
Hindi eight, Marathi fourteen, Telugu fifteen.

196
00:10:01,224 --> 00:10:02,323
And Sarvam's?

197
00:10:02,323 --> 00:10:05,275
Hindi, eight — exactly the same as English.

198
00:10:05,275 --> 00:10:06,625
Marathi, ten.

199
00:10:06,625 --> 00:10:08,009
Telugu, eleven.

200
00:10:08,009 --> 00:10:10,128
And this isn't one lucky sentence.

201
00:10:10,128 --> 00:10:15,631
We ran the same test on more than a thousand sentences, each one in thirteen languages.

202
00:10:15,631 --> 00:10:16,785
Look at Odia.

203
00:10:16,785 --> 00:10:20,649
On DeepSeek's chopper, five and a half times the pieces of English.

204
00:10:20,649 --> 00:10:23,203
On Sarvam's... less than one and a half.

205
00:10:23,203 --> 00:10:25,920
And BharatGen's chopper does about as well.

206
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:29,344
So for your language, the gap shrinks, a lot.

207
00:10:29,344 --> 00:10:32,864
Because this time... an Indian team put the missing pieces in.

208
00:10:33,730 --> 00:10:35,522
And it's already at work.

209
00:10:35,522 --> 00:10:40,177
Sarvam says its models help run Aadhaar services in Indian languages.

210
00:10:40,177 --> 00:10:46,807
And in October, Maharashtra gave Sarvam's A.I. assistant to two thousand five hundred government officials.

211
00:10:46,807 --> 00:10:50,095
So has India built its own Chat G.P.T.?

212
00:10:50,095 --> 00:10:51,600
Let me be honest with you.

213
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:54,155
Not one that matches the best in the world.

214
00:10:54,155 --> 00:10:55,003
Not yet.

215
00:10:55,003 --> 00:11:00,243
DeepSeek's newest big model has one point six trillion of those dials in total.

216
00:11:00,243 --> 00:11:02,234
About fifteen times Sarvam's.

217
00:11:02,234 --> 00:11:05,039
And Sarvam says it plainly, in its own report.

218
00:11:05,039 --> 00:11:11,123
The newest models from other labs are trained on far bigger clusters, with far bigger budgets.

219
00:11:11,123 --> 00:11:12,711
Here's the real scoreboard.

220
00:11:12,711 --> 00:11:14,669
India has the teachers.

221
00:11:14,669 --> 00:11:16,957
India has started on the library.

222
00:11:16,957 --> 00:11:18,422
And for the reading room...

223
00:11:18,422 --> 00:11:20,660
India still rents the chips.

224
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:21,641
What's next?

225
00:11:21,641 --> 00:11:28,393
The IndiaAI Mission has now picked twenty Indian proposals to build models, Sarvam and BharatGen among them.

226
00:11:28,393 --> 00:11:33,638
And BharatGen's mandate is to keep scaling, all the way up to a trillion dials.

227
00:11:33,638 --> 00:11:35,201
So here's the lesson.

228
00:11:35,201 --> 00:11:39,459
A machine speaks best in the language of the people who build it.

229
00:11:39,459 --> 00:11:47,921
If India only rents other countries' machines, you and I will keep paying extra to be understood... in our own languages.

230
00:11:47,921 --> 00:11:57,171
But today, there's an Indian machine that reads your Hindi, Marathi, Telugu, Bengali or Odia more cheaply than Chat G.P.T. and DeepSeek do.

231
00:11:57,171 --> 00:11:59,251
So what does this mean for you?

232
00:11:59,251 --> 00:12:03,073
First, check your Chat G.P.T. plan before the free year ends.

233
00:12:03,073 --> 00:12:06,381
Once it's over, it can start charging you on its own.

234
00:12:07,113 --> 00:12:13,666
And before you pay for any A.I., ask the same question in your own language to two or three of them.

235
00:12:13,666 --> 00:12:19,255
Chat G.P.T., DeepSeek, and an Indian one, like Sarvam's app, called Indus.

236
00:12:19,255 --> 00:12:22,656
Pay only for the one that really understands you.

237
00:12:22,656 --> 00:12:26,580
And A.I. isn't the only thing India has built for itself.

238
00:12:26,580 --> 00:12:31,143
Did you know India has its own satellite navigation, up in space?

239
00:12:31,143 --> 00:12:32,765
It's called Navik.

240
00:12:32,765 --> 00:12:36,902
So why does your phone still lean on America's G.P.S.?

241
00:12:36,902 --> 00:12:38,595
That's our next story.

242
00:12:38,595 --> 00:12:40,878
Before you go, try this tonight.

243
